I'm afraid there's no way to animate CSS properties directly. You have to use JavaScript to change the value of the property over time. Here's an example:

```
<div id="asd"></div>
<script>
var div = document.getElementById('asd');
setInterval(function(){
    div.style.left = parseInt(div.style.left || 0) + 10 + 'px';
}, 10);
</script>
<style>
#asd{
    position:absolute;
    width:100px;
    height:100px;
    background:red;
}
</style>
```

The problem is that you're trying to animate an `img` element which doesn't have any position set. You need to either:

- Set the `position` of the `img` to `relative`, `absolute` or `fixed`.
- Wrap the `img` in a `div` and animate the `div`.

Here's how you can do it:

```html
<div id="game">
  <div id="asd-wrapper">
    <img id="asd" src="http://i.imgur.com/ApuSWMC.png" alt="torch"/>
  </div>
</div>
```

```css
#asd-wrapper {
  position: relative;
}
```

```javascript
$('#asd-wrapper').click(function () {
  $(this).animate({
    left: "+=100px"
  }, 'fast');
});
```

